{"step_index":75,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-22T11:40:22Z","input_tokens":2569,"cache_read_tokens":42058,"output_tokens":1182,"thinking":"Currently, a download is in progress; its completion time is contingent upon the connection speed, but it doesn't require immediate attention. The process can continue in the background. Retaining the downloaded component will be advantageous for future tasks. The next steps will determine how to present this.\n\n\nInitial focus is on the fundamental architectural difference between C++ MMORPGs and Rust, primarily addressing the challenge of cyclical mutable references in C++ that violate Rust's ownership rules. The strategy involves employing an [ECS](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev) (like Bevy) for client-side/world simulation and [Data-Oriented Packet Codecs](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev) (like `serde`) on the network layer as a solution. This approach aims to reconcile the object graph inherent in the existing C++ code with Rust's memory management.\n\nNext, the roadmap is being formulated into distinct phases. The first phase targets understanding the game loop dynamics which encompasses `delta_time`, fixed update loops, and coordinate systems, essential for MMO determinism. Understanding 2.5D isometric math is also necessary for this phase, this will include conversion between 2D Cartesian world.\n\n\nDepth sorting in 2.5D requires dynamic sprite sorting based on the base/foot position (Z = -Y) to achieve the correct visual layering. This is crucial for isometric projections.\n\nPhase 2 will involve reverse-engineering the C++ packet structure to create a shared Rust crate that handles packet parsing and validation using safe parsing libraries. This crate will be essential for the network communication layer.\n\nPhase 3 focuses on implementing the 2.5D client architecture using a specific game engine. This includes isometric tilemap generation, entity animation, and isometric input handling with A* pathfinding.\n\nPhase 4 entails developing an authoritative headless server with a TCP/UDP networking protocol (TCP for reliability, UDP for speed) incorporating Area of Interest (AOI) to optimize network traffic, and client-side interpolation. This will include server reconciliation.\n\nPhase 5 will deal with implementing subsystems.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"status","TaskId":"99ede857-2b67-4a63-b995-20a566830cc5/task-72"}}]}
