{"step_index":77,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-22T11:40:36Z","input_tokens":3615,"cache_read_tokens":42602,"output_tokens":1884,"content":"# Roadmap: Porting a 2.5D MMORPG from C++ to Rust\n\nYour learning workspace has been configured to support this multi-session project:\n- [MISSION.md](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev/MISSION.md): Captures your concrete objective, success criteria, and constraints.\n- [RESOURCES.md](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev/RESOURCES.md): High-trust reference guides for ECS, networking, and 2.5D rendering.\n- [0001-prior-knowledge-and-project-scope.md](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev/learning-records/0001-prior-knowledge-and-project-scope.md): Initial baseline capturing your Rust experience and target architecture.\n- [NOTES.md](file:///home/derek/projects/game-dev/NOTES.md): Environmental notes including NixOS dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## The Paradigm Shift: C++ OOP vs. Rust ECS\n\nClassic C++ MMORPG codebases are almost always built around an **Object-Oriented pointer web**:\n* `Player*` holds a pointer to `Map*`, `Guild*`, and targeted `Monster*`.\n* `Monster*` holds a pointer to its aggro target `Player*`.\n* `Map*` holds collections of `Entity*`.\n\nIn Rust, cyclical mutable references (`&mut`) violate single-ownership and aliasing guarantees. Trying to port C++ pointer graphs directly into Rust leads to perpetual borrow checker battles or wrapping everything in messy `Rc<RefCell<T>>` / `Arc<RwLock<T>>`.\n\n**The Solution:**\n1. **Entity Component System (ECS)** (using [`bevy`](https://bevyengine.org)): Entities are plain 64-bit integer IDs. Components are pure data structs (`Position`, `Velocity`, `Health`). Systems are functions that query components without holding long-lived references.\n2. **Data-Oriented Network Protocol**: Strongly typed enums and structs with zero-copy binary serialization to match the C++ wire format.\n\n---\n\n## The 5-Phase Roadmap\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    P1[\"Phase 1: Game Loop & 2.5D Isometric Math\"] --> P2[\"Phase 2: Protocol Codec & Shared Crate\"]\n    P2 --> P3[\"Phase 3: 2.5D Client & Map Rendering\"]\n    P3 --> P4[\"Phase 4: Authoritative Headless Server\"]\n    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Gameplay Subsystems & Persistence\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Game Loop Fundamentals & 2.5D Isometric Math\n*Goal: Understand real-time simulation mechanics and coordinate transformations without getting overwhelmed by engine boilerplate.*\n\n1. **Game Loop Anatomy**:\n   * Fixed tick rates (e.g., 20Hz or 50Hz server ticks) vs. variable render framerate (`delta_time`).\n   * Accumulator loops: Why server game logic must be deterministic and decoupled from rendering speed.\n2. **2.5D Isometric Coordinate Systems**:\n   * **Cartesian Grid** $(x, y) \\leftrightarrow$ **Screen Coordinates** $(sx, sy)$:\n     $$\\text{screen\\_x} = (x - y) \\cdot \\frac{\\text{tile\\_width}}{2}$$\n     $$\\text{screen\\_y} = (x + y) \\cdot \\frac{\\text{tile\\_height}}{2}$$\n   * Inverse projection: Screen cursor pick to tile index.\n3. **Depth Sorting (Y-Sorting)**:\n   * How 2.5D games create depth: Dynamically sorting render order by base foot position ($Z = -Y$).\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Protocol Extraction & Shared Crate\n*Goal: Establish a single `protocol` crate that both client and server will share to communicate.*\n\n1. **Deconstruct C++ Packets**:\n   * Inspect existing C++ packet structures (header, opcode, body length, payload).\n   * Map raw C++ structs to Rust `#[repr(C)]` or `#[repr(packed)]` structs, or define idiomatic Rust enums with [`serde`](https://github.com/serde-rs/serde) and [`bincode`](https://github.com/bincode-org/bincode).\n2. **Binary Deserialization & Framing**:\n   * Handling stream boundaries and packet fragmentation.\n   * Writing unit tests that feed raw hexadecimal packet dumps captured from the C++ game and verify they deserialize into correct Rust types.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: The 2.5D Client Prototype ([`bevy`](https://bevyengine.org))\n*Goal: A playable local client where you can navigate the isometric map with proper depth and animations.*\n\n1. **Map Loading & Isometric Tilemaps**:\n   * Parse the existing map/tile format from the C++ project.\n   * Render layered tiles (ground, obstacles, decorative overlays) using [`bevy_ecs_tilemap`](https://github.com/StarArawn/bevy_ecs_tilemap) or custom quad batches.\n2. **Character Controller & Animations**:\n   * Sprite sheets with 4-way or 8-way directional walks.\n   * Isometric pathfinding (A* algorithm on walkable grid cells).\n   * Smooth movement interpolation between tile centers.\n3. **Camera System**:\n   * Smooth following camera constrained to map boundaries.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Authoritative Server & Real-Time Sync\n*Goal: Two client windows connecting to a headless server, seeing each other move on the isometric grid.*\n\n1. **Headless Server Architecture**:\n   * Standalone Rust binary with no GPU/window dependencies.\n   * Network transport using [`renet`](https://github.com/lucaspoffo/renet) or async TCP/UDP sockets via [`tokio`](https://tokio.rs).\n2. **Authoritative Movement & Input Validation**:\n   * Client sends move requests; server validates collision and broadcasts position.\n   * Client-side prediction and snapshot interpolation to eliminate perceived lag.\n3. **Area of Interest (AOI)**:\n   * Spatial hashing / grid partitioning on the server so clients only receive updates for entities within their visibility range (essential for MMO scalability).\n\n---\n\n### Phase 5: Porting Gameplay Subsystems & World Persistence\n*Goal: Porting the complex game rules from the C++ codebase.*\n\n1. **Combat & Stats Pipeline**:\n   * Translating C++ damage formulas, buffs, status effects, and cooldown timers into ECS components and systems.\n2. **Inventory & Items**:\n   * Transactional equipment and trade logic (ensuring item duplication bugs are impossible by design).\n3. **NPC & Mob AI**:\n   * Server-side finite state machines (FSM) or Behavior Trees for aggro, patrolling, and attacking.\n4. **Data Persistence**:\n   * Saving character progress, inventory, and accounts with an embedded engine like [`sqlite`](https://github.com/rusqlite/rusqlite) or asynchronous [`sqlx`](https://github.com/launchbadge/sqlx).\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Next Step\n\nWhen you are ready, we will generate **Lesson 0001: 2.5D Isometric Math & The Game Loop**, complete with an interactive visualizer for coordinate transforms and an interactive verification quiz. \n\nWould you like to start Lesson 0001, or do you have specific details about your C++ project (e.g., networking protocol, map format, or art pipeline) you'd like to share first?","thinking":"Task 72 is progressing; the Nix compiler packages are downloading. This will enable compilation of project dependencies. The goal is to establish a working development environment as defined in the project scope.\n\n\n"}
